    $u93364663${"version":3,"generated_at":"2026-11-15T11:46:23+00:00","head":"103eb2bb6e534a77c8f3a248cf54f5ac2147682e","tour":{"overview":"udebt measures \"understanding debt\": how much of a codebase a developer has lost track of as it changed, which matters most for code an AI wrote. The CLI and local analysis pipeline is the entry point. It uses Structural unit discovery to split a repo into units, Provenance and responsibility to see who wrote what, and Debt scoring, weights and decay to turn history into a score. The LLM backend and prompts write the quiz questions. The Teaching layer and local web server help people pay the debt down. The Frozen JSON contract and sync, the Supabase schema and loaders, the Hosted analysis worker (Render) and the Hosted web app (Netlify) carry the same ideas to a hosted service, and the Python/TS parity contract keeps the two languages in agreement. A superseded hosted API draft sits aside.","sections":[{"unit_id":"structural-discovery","paragraph":"This unit proposes the system's units automatically, so every other part has something to measure. It builds two graphs, one of which files import which and one of which files change together in history. It then partitions them into groups. It sits underneath most of the system: Debt scoring, Provenance and responsibility, the CLI, the hosted worker, the parity tests and the Teaching layer all consume its output. It also depends on scoring and provenance in return. The decision was community detection (Louvain) over the merged graphs, in two levels. Directory-based units were not chosen, nor was asking an LLM alone, because the aim is units grounded in code and history. Per the history, an AI agent (Claude Code) wrote both commits on 2026-11-12 [ai]. The history does not record any debate about the choice."},{"unit_id":"provenance-responsibility","paragraph":"This unit reads git history to work out who wrote what, which decisions were made, and which developer is responsible for which unit. It uses Structural unit discovery for the units, plus scoring, the LLM backend and the CLI. In turn the CLI, scoring, the Teaching layer, the hosted worker and the parity tests use it. The decision is that understanding debt belongs to a person, so each developer gets their own scope, code outside that scope counts as an OUTSIDE risk, and a developer's own commits carry a prior. Decision candidates are mined from history. Ignoring authorship, or keeping hand-written ownership files, was rejected because AI-authored code is the focus. Claude Code wrote all three commits on 2026-11-12 [ai]. The history gives no further reasoning."},{"unit_id":"teaching-layer","paragraph":"This unit turns measuring debt into paying it down. It offers architecture tours, a tutor, a spaced review queue, teach-back exercises, change digests and a browser interface started by the serve command. It uses the CLI, scoring, the LLM backend, Provenance and responsibility and Structural unit discovery. The CLI, the hosted worker, the LLM backend and the parity tests depend on it. The decision was to put spaced review and walkthroughs on top of the existing quiz ladder, served by a small standard-library HTTP server with plain static JavaScript. A terminal-only tool and a framework web app were rejected; the history does not spell out why beyond the goal of learning. Claude Code wrote all three commits between 2026-11-14 and 2026-11-15 [ai]."},{"unit_id":"llm-backend","paragraph":"This unit puts every model call behind one interface and holds the prompts for quiz questions and orientations. It sits between the CLI, scoring, the Teaching layer, Provenance and the hosted worker, which all ask it for text. It also depends on the CLI, scoring and the Teaching layer, so the dependencies run both ways. The decision was to run the subscription-billed claude command-line tool, with deny rules on the tool's own records folder and no repository settings or MCP servers. A fake backend that keys on task markers in the prompt lets tests run offline. Calling the Anthropic API with a key was not chosen, because that needs a key and costs money. Skipping isolation was not chosen either, because it could leak reference answers or run a hostile repository's hooks. Claude Code wrote the three commits, 2026-11-11 to 2026-11-14 [ai]."},{"unit_id":"json-contract","paragraph":"This unit defines the versioned JSON formats the CLI writes and the hosted side reads. It validates them with a small standard-library validator and pushes and pulls records to the hosted store. It uses the CLI and scoring for what it describes, and is used by the CLI, scoring and the hosted worker. The decision is permissive: extra properties are allowed, the version changes only when a field is removed or retyped, and the map is cut down to metadata before upload so reference answers never leave the machine. A shared database schema, protobuf and having no versioning were rejected, because the goal is to decouple the CLI from the hosted service. Claude Code wrote both commits on 2026-11-15 [ai]. The history does not record deeper reasoning."},{"unit_id":"debt-model","paragraph":"This unit computes the understanding-debt score for a unit. It also holds the commit-weighting rules, change risk, caching, history replay over time, the synthetic-developer experiment and the quiz ladder levels. It uses the CLI, Provenance, Structural unit discovery, the LLM backend and the contract. The CLI, Teaching layer, contract, hosted worker, parity tests and others use it. The decision is a heuristic. Lockfile and version-only edits count for less, a commit's lines are capped at the unit's size, and understanding decays by the share of the unit changed, at a separate rate for each ladder level. Raw line counts, a single decay rate and a learned model were not used. Rates were refit from the synthetic-developer experiments. Claude Code wrote all eight commits, 2026-11-12 to 2026-11-15 [ai]."},{"unit_id":"supabase-schema","paragraph":"This unit is the hosted database: a Postgres schema for Supabase, a SQLite twin for tests, hosted migrations, an idempotent loader and the sample data as load SQL. It uses the CLI and the hosted worker, and both of them use it. It also changes together with the Hosted web app. The decision was Supabase Postgres with row-level security, plus the SQLite twin and an idempotent loader. An API with its own database, as in the earlier drafts, was rejected, as was NoSQL, because Supabase supplies authentication, row-level security and storage. Claude Code wrote all three commits on 2026-11-15 [ai]. The history does not say anything about why NoSQL was ruled out beyond that."},{"unit_id":"hosted-analysis","paragraph":"This unit is the server-side worker. It analyses a repository in a temporary clone, runs an LLM tool loop as a Render service, handles GitHub webhooks and pull-request checks, and writes results to Supabase. It reuses the CLI, LLM backend, scoring, Provenance, Structural discovery, the Teaching layer, the contract and the Supabase schema. The CLI, the parity tests and the schema use it in turn. The decision was a Python worker that reuses the CLI code, calls the model through the messages API, talks to Supabase over REST and uses a GitHub app. Serverless-only analysis was rejected because long clones do not fit Netlify functions. The earlier hosted API draft was superseded. Claude Code wrote all five commits on 2026-11-15 [ai]."},{"unit_id":"cli-pipeline","paragraph":"This is the local udebt command-line tool. It maps a git repo into units, weighs commits, scores understanding and writes records as files in a hidden folder inside the repo. It is the hub of the system. It calls scoring, the LLM backend, Provenance, the Teaching layer, Structural discovery, the hosted worker, the contract and the schema, and most of them call back into it. The decision was a Python CLI using only the standard library, with file-based state and git as its only data source. A server-first app, a database and third-party dependencies were rejected, so the tool is local-first and needs no install; the hosted side came later. Claude Code wrote all three commits on 2026-11-11 and 2026-11-12 [ai]."},{"unit_id":"web-app","paragraph":"This is the hosted browser app. People sign in, view specs, take the quiz ladder, read walkthroughs and see debt and review. It sits at the edge of the system, with no listed users or uses. It changes together with the parity tests, the Supabase schema and the hosted worker, which is how it stays in step with them. The decision was a vanilla TypeScript single-page app built with Vite, taken from the HintWise template, with Supabase sign-in, Netlify serverless functions, user-supplied model keys and a content security policy. A framework such as React and server rendering were rejected. The stated reason is reuse of an existing template; the history shows nothing more. Claude Code wrote all three commits on 2026-11-15 [ai]."},{"unit_id":"parity-tests","paragraph":"This unit keeps the Python and TypeScript implementations behaving the same. It consists of shared prompt parts and fixture-based tests on both sides. It uses scoring, Structural discovery, the hosted worker, the LLM backend, Provenance and the Teaching layer as the reference behaviour. It changes together with the web app, the worker and the schema. The decision was to implement the logic twice and check both against generated shared fixtures, rather than run one runtime. Calling Python from the web and rewriting everything in one language were rejected, because the hosted functions cannot run the Python CLI. Claude Code wrote all three commits on 2026-11-15 [ai]. The history does not record whether other options were weighed."},{"unit_id":"drafts-hosted-api","paragraph":"This is a parked first draft of a hosted API service, with row-level-security migrations. It is not part of the running system, and no connections to other units were found. It was kept under the drafts folder as a reference rather than deleted. The design in HOSTING.md replaced it with the Supabase and Netlify approach. The history does not say why it was kept instead of removed. The later hosted worker and schema are the live versions of the same ideas. Claude Code parked it in a single commit on 2026-11-15 [ai]. Treat it as background reading only and do not build on it."}],"basis":"d5588b053d74","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788","generated_at":"2026-11-15T11:50:48+00:00"},"units":[{"id":"cli-pipeline","name":"CLI and local analysis pipeline","kind":"module","summary":"The `udebt` CLI that maps a repo into units, weighs commits, scores understanding and writes records under .udebt/.","decision":"Stdlib-only Python CLI with file-based state in .udebt/ and git as the only data source.","alternatives":"A server-first app; a database; third-party dependencies.","why":"Local-first, zero-install tool; the hosted side was added later.","paths":["udebt/cli.py","udebt/mapping.py","udebt/store.py","udebt/repo.py","udebt/quiz.py","udebt/dig.py","udebt/sizes.py","udebt/units.py"],"centrality":5,"clusters":["C1","C1.1","C2.1"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"5fafc04","date":"2026-11-11","klass":"ai","agent":"claude-code","author":"Claude","subject":"Add udebt vertical slice: map, quiz, status, dig over a git repo"},{"sha":"3b3f9a1","date":"2026-11-11","klass":"ai","agent":"claude-code","author":"Claude","subject":"Warn when dig returns no check question; enforce map unit cap"},{"sha":"dd26ff4","date":"2026-11-12","klass":"ai","agent":"claude-code","author":"Claude","subject":"Resolve the mapper's paths against the tracked files"}],"importance":1.0,"orientation":{"purpose":"The local `udebt` command-line tool. It maps a git repo into units, weighs commits, scores how well someone understands each unit, and writes the results as records under .udebt/.","decision":"A stdlib-only Python CLI that keeps state in files 