ted-api","what":"the draft service and migrations"}],"shaped_by":"[ai] Claude (claude-code) parked it in one commit on 2026-11-15.","basis":"117153bacab5","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788","generated_at":"2026-11-15T11:50:21+00:00"},"files":["drafts/hosted-api/app.py","drafts/hosted-api/jobs.py","drafts/hosted-api/reads.py","drafts/hosted-api/importer.py"]}],"connections":[["cli-pipeline","debt-model","uses",18.0],["cli-pipeline","hosted-analysis","uses",2.0],["cli-pipeline","json-contract","uses",2.0],["cli-pipeline","llm-backend","uses",12.0],["cli-pipeline","provenance-responsibility","uses",11.0],["cli-pipeline","structural-discovery","uses",3.0],["cli-pipeline","supabase-schema","uses",1.0],["cli-pipeline","teaching-layer","uses",9.0],["debt-model","cli-pipeline","uses",13.0],["debt-model","json-contract","uses",1.0],["debt-model","llm-backend","uses",2.0],["debt-model","provenance-responsibility","uses",9.0],["debt-model","structural-discovery","uses",4.0],["hosted-analysis","cli-pipeline","uses",8.0],["hosted-analysis","debt-model","uses",2.5],["hosted-analysis","json-contract","uses",1.0],["hosted-analysis","llm-backend","uses",4.5],["hosted-analysis","provenance-responsibility","uses",2.0],["hosted-analysis","structural-discovery","uses",2.0],["hosted-analysis","supabase-schema","uses",1.0],["hosted-analysis","teaching-layer","uses",2.0],["json-contract","cli-pipeline","uses",5.0],["json-contract","debt-model","uses",3.0],["llm-backend","cli-pipeline","uses",4.0],["llm-backend","debt-model","uses",2.0],["llm-backend","teaching-layer","uses",1.0],["parity-tests","debt-model","uses",2.5],["parity-tests","hosted-analysis","uses",1.5],["parity-tests","llm-backend","uses",1.5],["parity-tests","provenance-responsibility","uses",1.0],["parity-tests","structural-discovery","uses",2.0],["parity-tests","teaching-layer","uses",1.0],["provenance-responsibility","cli-pipeline","uses",5.0],["provenance-responsibility","debt-model","uses",2.0],["provenance-responsibility","llm-backend","uses",1.0],["provenance-responsibility","structural-discovery","uses",1.0],["structural-discovery","debt-model","uses",1.0],["structural-discovery","provenance-responsibility","uses",1.0],["supabase-schema","cli-pipeline","uses",1.0],["supabase-schema","hosted-analysis","uses",1.0],["teaching-layer","cli-pipeline","uses",14.0],["teaching-layer","debt-model","uses",12.0],["teaching-layer","llm-backend","uses",11.0],["teaching-layer","provenance-responsibility","uses",6.0],["teaching-layer","structural-discovery","uses",2.0],["cli-pipeline","debt-model","together",10.34],["cli-pipeline","hosted-analysis","together",2.92],["cli-pipeline","json-contract","together",1.26],["cli-pipeline","llm-backend","together",5.79],["cli-pipeline","parity-tests","together",0.2],["cli-pipeline","provenance-responsibility","together",3.24],["cli-pipeline","structural-discovery","together",0.53],["cli-pipeline","supabase-schema","together",1.2],["cli-pipeline","teaching-layer","together",2.98],["cli-pipeline","web-app","together",0.2],["debt-model","json-contract","together",0.42],["debt-model","llm-backend","together",3.67],["debt-model","provenance-responsibility","together",3.43],["debt-model","structural-discovery","together",1.03],["debt-model","teaching-layer","together",2.51],["hosted-analysis","llm-backend","together",0.2],["hosted-analysis","parity-tests","together",0.52],["hosted-analysis","supabase-schema","together",1.52],["hosted-analysis","web-app","together",0.52],["llm-backend","parity-tests","together",0.2],["llm-backend","provenance-responsibility","together",1.25],["llm-backend","supabase-schema","together",0.2],["llm-backend","teaching-layer","together",1.76],["llm-backend","web-app","together",0.2],["parity-tests","supabase-schema","together",0.52],["parity-tests","web-app","together",1.47],["provenance-responsibility","structural-discovery","together",1.03],["provenance-responsibility","teaching-layer","together",0.33],["supabase-schema","web-app","together",1.33]],"hubs":[],"order":["structural-discovery","provenance-responsibility","teaching-layer","llm-backend","json-contract","debt-model","supabase-schema","hosted-analysis","cli-pipeline","web-app","parity-tests","drafts-hosted-api"],"tour":{"markdown":"# Architecture tour: understanding-debt\n\nWritten by `udebt tour` at `e9c1c8c5` on 2026-11-15; 12 units, about 10 minutes to read. Units come in the order to learn them: each after the units it uses.\n\nudebt measures \"understanding debt\": how much of a codebase a developer has lost track of as it changed, which matters most for code an AI wrote. The CLI and local analysis pipeline is the entry point. It uses Structural unit discovery to split a repo into units, Provenance and responsibility to see who wrote what, and Debt scoring, weights and decay to turn history into a score. The LLM backend and prompts write the quiz questions. The Teaching layer and local web server help people pay the debt down. The Frozen JSON contract and sync, the Supabase schema and loaders, the Hosted analysis worker (Render) and the Hosted web app (Netlify) carry the same ideas to a hosted service, and the Python/TS parity contract keeps the two languages in agreement. A superseded hosted API draft sits aside.\n\n## The map\n\n*Uses*: its code imports the other unit's (strongest first). *Used by*: how many units import it. *Changes with*: the units commits most often touch with it.\n\n1. **Structural unit discovery** (`structural-discovery`): uses `debt-model`, `provenance-responsibility`; used by 6; changes with `debt-model`, `provenance-responsibility`\n2. **Provenance and responsibility** (`provenance-responsibility`): uses `cli-pipeline`, `debt-model`, `llm-backend`, `structural-discovery`; used by 6; changes with `debt-model`, `cli-pipeline`\n3. **Teaching layer and local web server** (`teaching-layer`): uses `cli-pipeline`, `debt-model`, `llm-backend`, `provenance-responsibility`, `structural-discovery`; used by 4; changes with `cli-pipeline`, `debt-model`\n4. **LLM backend and prompts** (`llm-backend`): uses `cli-pipeline`, `debt-model`, `teaching-layer`; used by 6; changes with `cli-pipeline`, `debt-model`\n5. **Frozen JSON contract and sync** (`json-contract`): uses `cli-pipeline`, `debt-model`; used by 3; changes with `cli-pipeline`, `debt-model`\n6. **Debt scoring, weights and decay** (`debt-model`): uses `cli-pipeline`, `provenance-responsibility`, `structural-discovery`, `llm-backend`, `json-contract`; used by 8; changes with `cli-pipeline`, `llm-backend`\n7. **Supabase schema and loaders** (`supabase-schema`): uses `cli-pipeline`, `hosted-analysis`; used by 2; changes with `hosted-analysis`, `web-app`\n8. **Hosted analysis worker (Render)** (`hosted-analysis`): uses `cli-pipeline`, `llm-backend`, `debt-model`, `provenance-responsibility`, `structural-discovery`, `teaching-layer`, `json-contract`, `supabase-schema`; used by 3; changes with `cli-pipeline`, `supabase-schema`\n9. **CLI and local analysis pipeline** (`cli-pipeline`): uses `debt-model`, `llm-backend`, `provenance-responsibility`, `teaching-layer`, `structural-discovery`, `hosted-analysis`, `json-contract`, `supabase-schema`; used by 7; changes with `debt-model`, `llm-backend`\n10. **Hosted web app (Netlify)** (`web-app`): changes with `parity-tests`, `supabase-schema`\n11. **Python/TS parity contract** (`parity-tests`): uses `debt-model`, `structural-discovery`, `hosted-analysis`, `llm-backend`, `provenance-responsibility`, `teaching-layer`; changes with `web-app`, `hosted-analysis`\n12. **Superseded hosted API draft** (`drafts-hosted-api`): stands alone\n\n## 1. Structural unit discovery (`structural-discovery`)\n\nThis unit proposes the system's units automatically, so every other part has something to measure. It builds two graphs, one of which files import which and one of which files change together in history. It then partitions them into groups. It sits underneath most of the system: Debt scoring, Provenance and responsibility, the CLI, the hosted worker, the parity tests and the Teaching layer all consume its output. It also depends on scoring and provenance in return. The decision was community detection (Louvain) over the merged graphs, in two levels. Directory-based units were not chosen, nor was asking an LLM alone, because the aim is units grounded in code and history. Per the history, an AI agent (Claude Code) wrote both commits on 2026-11-12 [ai]. The history does not record any debate about the choice.\n\nOpen first: `udebt/structure.py`, `udebt/graph.py`, `udebt/imports.py`.\n\n## 2. Provenance and responsibility (`provenance-responsibility`)\n\nThis unit reads git history to work out who wrote what, which decisions were made, and which developer is responsible for which unit. It uses Structural unit discovery for the units, plus scoring, the LLM backend and the CLI. In turn the CLI, scoring, the Teaching layer, the hosted worker and the parity tests use it. The decision is that understanding debt belongs to a person, so each developer gets their own scope, code outside that scope counts as an OUTSIDE risk, and a developer's own commits carry a prior. Decision candidates are mined from history. Ignoring authorship, or keeping hand-written ownership files, was rejected because AI-authored code is the focus. Claude Code wrote all three commits on 2026-11-12 [ai]. The history gives no further reasoning.\n\nOpen first: `udebt/provenance.py`, `udebt/responsibility.py`, `udebt/decisions.py`.\n\n## 3. Teaching layer and local web server (`teaching-layer`)\n\nThis unit turns measuring debt into paying it down. It offers architecture tours, a tutor, a spaced review queue, teach-back exercises, change digests and a browser interface started by the serve command. It uses the CLI, scoring, the LLM backend, Provenance and responsibility and Structural unit discovery. The CLI, the hosted worker, the LLM backend and the parity tests depend on it. The decision was to put spaced review and walkthroughs on top of the existing quiz ladder, served by a small standard-library HTTP server with plain static JavaScript. A terminal-only tool and a framework web app were rejected; the history does not spell out why beyond the goal of learning. Claude Code wrote all three commits between 2026-11-14 and 2026-11-15 [ai].\n\nOpen first: `udebt/serve.py`, `udebt/review.py`, `udebt/tour.py`.\n\n## 4. LLM backend and prompts (`llm-backend`)\n\nThis unit puts every model call behind one interface and holds the prompts for quiz questions and orientations. It sits between the CLI, scoring, the Teaching layer, Provenance and the hosted worker, which all ask it for text. It also depends on the CLI, scoring and the Teaching layer, so the dependencies run both ways. The decision was to run the subscription-billed claude command-line tool, with deny rules on the tool's own records folder and no repository settings or MCP servers. A fake backend that keys on task markers in the prompt lets tests run offline. Calling the Anthropic API with a key was not chosen, because that needs a key and costs money. Skipping isolation was not chosen either, because it could leak reference answers or run a hostile repository's hooks. Claude Code wrote the three commits, 2026-11-11 to 2026-11-14 [ai].\n\nOpen first: `udebt/llm.py`, `udebt/prompts.py`, `tests/test_llm_isolation.py`.\n\n## 5. Frozen JSON contract and sync (`json-contract`)\n\nThis unit defines the versioned JSON formats the CLI writes and the hosted side reads. It validates them with a small standard-library validator and pushes and pulls records to the hosted store. It uses the CLI and scoring for what it describes, and is used by the CLI, scoring and the hosted worker. The decision is permissive: extra properties are allowed, the version changes only when a field is removed or retyped, and the map is cut down to metadata before upload so reference answers never leave the machine. A shared database schema, protobuf and having no versioning were rejected, because the goal is to decouple the CLI from the hosted service. Claude Code wrote both commits on 2026-11-15 [ai]. The history does not record deeper reasoning.\n\nOpen first: `udebt/contract.py`, `udebt/remote.py`, `tests/test_contract.py`.\n\n## 6. Debt scoring, weights and decay (`debt-model`)\n\nThis unit computes the understanding-debt score for a unit. It also holds the commit-weighting rules, change risk, caching, history replay over time, the synthetic-developer experiment and the quiz ladder levels. It uses the CLI, Provenance, Structural unit discovery, the LLM backend and the contract. The CLI, Teaching layer, contract, hosted worker, parity tests and others use it. The decision is a heuristic. Lockfile and version-only edits count for less, a commit's lines are capped at the unit's size, and understanding decays by the share of the unit changed, at a separate rate for each ladder level. Raw line counts, a single decay rate and a learned model were not used. Rates were refit from the synthetic-developer experiments. Claude Code wrote all eight commits, 2026-11-12 to 2026-11-15 [ai].\n\nOpen first: `udebt/scoring.py`, `udebt/weights.py`, `udebt/ladder.py`.\n\n## 7. Supabase schema and loaders (`supabase-schema`)\n\nThis unit is the hosted database: a Postgres schema for Supabase, a SQLite twin for tests, hosted migrations, an idempotent loader and the sample data as load SQL. It uses the CLI and the hosted worker, and both of them use it. It also changes together with the Hosted web app. The decision was Supabase Postgres with row-level security, plus the SQLite twin and an idempotent loader. An API with its own database, as in the earlier drafts, was rejected, as was NoSQL, because Supabase supplies authentication, row-level security and storage. Claude Code wrote all three commits on 2026-11-15 [ai]. The history does not say anything about why NoSQL was ruled out beyond that.\n\nOpen first: `supabase`, `udebt/loadsql.py`, `samples`.\n\n## 8. Hosted analysis worker (Render) (`hosted-analysis`)\n\nThis unit is the server-side worker. It analyses a repository in a temporary clone, runs an LLM tool loop as a Render service, handles GitHub webhooks and pull-request checks, and writes results to Supabase. It reuses the CLI, LLM backend, scoring, Provenance, Structural discovery, the Teaching layer, the contract and the Supabase schema. The CLI, the parity tests and the schema use it in turn. The decision was a Python worker that reuses the CLI code, calls the model through the messages API, talks to Supabase over REST and uses a GitHub app. Serverless-only analysis was rejected because long clones do not fit Netlify functions. The earlier hosted API draft was superseded. Claude Code wrote all five commits on 2026-11-15 [ai].\n\nOpen first: `udebt/worker.py`, `udebt/hosted/worker_service.py`, `render.yaml`.\n\n## 9. CLI and local analysis pipeline (`cli-pipeline`)\n\nThis is the local udebt command-line tool. It maps a git repo into units, weighs commits, scores understanding and writes records as files in a hidden folder inside the repo. It is the hub of the system. It calls scoring, the LLM backend, Provenance, the Teaching layer, Structural discovery, the hosted worker, the contract and the schema, and most of them call back into it. The decision was a Python CLI using only the standard library, with file-based state and git as its only data source. A server-first app, a database and third-party dependencies were rejected, so the tool is local-first and needs no install; the hosted side came later. Claude Code wrote all three commits on 2026-11-11 and 2026-11-12 [ai].\n\nOpen first: `udebt/cli.py`, `udebt/mapping.py`, `udebt/store.py`.\n\n## 10. Hosted web app (Netlify) (`web-app`)\n\nThis is the hosted browser app. People sign in, view specs, take the quiz ladder, read walkthroughs and see debt and review. It sits at the edge of the system, with no listed users or uses. It changes together with the parity tests, the Supabase schema and the hosted worker, which is how it stays in step with them. The decision was a vanilla TypeScript single-page app built with Vite, taken from the HintWise template, with Supabase sign-in, Netlify serverless functions, user-supplied model keys and a content security policy. A framework such as React and server rendering were rejected. The stated reason is reuse of an existing template; the history shows nothing more. Claude Code wrote all three commits on 2026-11-15 [ai].\n\nOpen first: `web`, `web/netlify/functions`, `netlify.toml`.\n\n## 11. Python/TS parity contract (`parity-tests`)\n\nThis unit keeps the Python and TypeScript implementations behaving the same. It consists of shared prompt parts and fixture-based tests on both sides. It uses scoring, Structural discovery, the hosted worker, the LLM backend, Provenance and the Teaching layer as the reference behaviour. It changes together with the web app, the worker and the schema. The decision was to implement the logic twice and check both against generated shared fixtures, rather than run one runtime. Calling Python from the web and rewriting everything in one language were rejected, because the hosted functions cannot run the Python CLI. Claude Code wrote all three commits on 2026-11-15 [ai]. The history does not record whether other options were weighed.\n\nOpen first: `tests/test_hosted_parity.py`, `web/test/parity.test.ts`, `udebt/hosted/prompt_parts.py`.\n\n## 12. Superseded hosted API draft (`drafts-hosted-api`)\n\nThis is a parked first draft of a hosted API service, with row-level-security migrations. It is not part of the running system, and no connections to other units were found. It was kept under the drafts folder as a reference rather than deleted. The design in HOSTING.md replaced it with the Supabase and Netlify approach. The history does not say why it was kept instead of removed. The later hosted worker and schema are the live versions of the same ideas. Claude Code parked it in a single commit on 2026-11-15 [ai]. Treat it as background reading only and do not build on it.\n\nOpen first: `drafts/hosted-api`.\n\n---\n\nNext: `udebt path` for the order to learn these in and the time each takes, `udebt dig <unit>` for a walkthrough of one with its code.\n","minutes":10},"walkthroughs":{"cli-pipeline":{"flow":"A developer runs `udebt status` in a repo that already has .udebt/ and a map, and gets the per-unit debt table and index.","steps":[{"file":"udebt/cli.py","start_line":548,"end_line":572,"narration":"`cmd_status` is the whole pipeline in miniature. It finds the repo, requires `.udebt/` and a saved map, then pulls commits, commit weights and the responsibility set before scoring. The three early commits by Claude (2026-11-11 and 11-12) built this shape. Everything here is read from local files and git, with no network call and no model call."},{"file":"udebt/store.py","start_line":176,"end_line":200,"narration":"The guards `require` and `require_map` are how file-based state fails. A missing `.udebt/` directory or a missing `map.json` stops the command with a one-line instruction (`udebt init`, `udebt map`). With no database, 'is the tool set up?' just means 'does this directory or file exist?'. The config is also re-read from `config.json` each time through the `config` property."},{"file":"udebt/mapping.py","start_line":55,"end_line":83,"narration":"git is the only data source. `commits_for` reads every commit reachable from HEAD through a parsed-commit cache kept under `.udebt/`, classifies each as human, assisted, ai or bot using configured signatures, and marks commits the developer directed. `weights_for` then turns commits into weights, cached in `commits.json`. It also marks version-bump files first, so later share calculations skip them."},{"file":"udebt/quiz.py","start_line":98,"end_line":125,"narration":"`compute_scores` is the scoring core. It takes the developer's quiz records, the commits and weights (reusing them if the caller passed them in), line counts for unit sizes if the decay model is 'share', and a ledger of which commit touched which unit. Passing `cutoff` replays the past, and leaving it out means 'now', which is what `status` does. The same function backs `quiz`, `dig`, `path` and the replay."},{"file":"udebt/store.py","start_line":203,"end_line":221,"narration":"Quiz evidence is an append-only JSONL file per developer, named from a slug of their email under `.udebt/records/`. `append_record` adds a timestamped line, and `load_records` re-reads the whole file and parses each line. Scoring is therefore a pure function of git history plus these lines. There is no locking and no index, which is fine for one local user."},{"file":"udebt/cli.py","start_line":603,"end_line":623,"narration":"Each unit's row combines the score (understanding, basis, debt) with fresh provenance numbers, namely the AI share and your share of the unit's mass. Rows are drawn with `print` and fixed-width formatting, with sub-units placed under their parents. The units come from the saved map and the numbers come from the commits, so a map saved earlier can show new numbers after new commits land."}],"trap":"A reader may assume `status` stores a computed debt figure or calls the model, but nothing is stored: debt is recomputed on every run from git history, the cached commit weights, and the append-only quiz records, with no LLM call.","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788"},"debt-model":{"flow":"A developer runs `status` (or a quiz) to see their debt on a unit that has one past quiz record and has since received several commits, including a lockfile bump and a big code change.","steps":[{"file":"udebt/weights.py","start_line":294,"end_line":320,"narration":"Each commit first gets a weight in [0,1] from cheap git signals. Lockfile/generated/media-only commits get 0.02, and manifest edits that only move version numbers get 0.03 (added in 4ff6ad0), so a bump barely counts as change. Claude wrote these rules during Phase 1 and later commits; the numbers are heuristics, not fitted values."},{"file":"udebt/weights.py","start_line":623,"end_line":636,"narration":"line_weights turns commit weights into the multiplier applied to a commit's changed lines in the share-changed decay. A commit from a merged branch gets the whole branch's summed weight (capped at 1), because the branch was weighed as one piece of work. Without this, a ten-commit branch would count about a tenth of what the same work squashed would."},{"file":"udebt/provenance.py","start_line":621,"end_line":640,"narration":"touches_of lists the commits that touch a unit, with the lines each changed there. It caps each commit's lines at the unit's size just after that commit (9111b03), so a rewrite counts as at most 100% of the unit, not old plus new lines. The size is a running sum of growth, so no tree reads are needed."},{"file":"udebt/quiz.py","start_line":117,"end_line":137,"narration":"The caller wires it together for each unit. It finds the last quiz record's commit and sums the weighted mass and weighted lines landed after it. It converts the lines to a share of the unit's size, then calls score_unit along with the own-commit prior. The share is None when no size is known."},{"file":"udebt/scoring.py","start_line":287,"end_line":316,"narration":"faded() holds the core of the model. It takes each ladder level's latest-sitting value, multiplies it by that level's own decay factor, and only then combines them: a third each for levels 1-3, plus 0.1 each for depth levels 4-5. So the same change erodes a level 5 pass faster than a level 3 pass (Phase 3.5, 4b37b20)."},{"file":"udebt/scoring.py","start_line":207,"end_line":215,"narration":"Decay.factor is where share becomes fade: exp(-k * share), with k from the level (0.2 for levels 1-3, 1.0 for levels 4-5). If no share is available it falls back to the older per-mass rule, which ignores the level. debt then follows as importance * (1 - understanding), and the debt index is an importance-weighted average over architectural units."}],"trap":"A reader may assume debt grows with raw commit or line counts and that every ladder level decays alike. In fact lines are weighted by commit type and capped at the unit's size, divided by unit size, and levels 1-3 decay about five times slower (k 0.2) than levels 4-5 (k 1.0).","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788"},"llm-backend":{"flow":"A developer runs a udebt command that needs an orientation for a unit (e.g. before a quiz), with the real backend selected, so orientation text is generated through one structured claude CLI call.","steps":[{"file":"udebt/llm.py","start_line":485,"end_line":488,"narration":"Every command gets its backend from this one factory. UDEBT_LLM=fake returns FakeLLM; otherwise ClaudeCLI. This is the only switch between offline tests and the subscription-billed CLI, so callers never know which they hold."},{"file":"udebt/prompts.py","start_line":616,"end_line":629,"narration":"The orientation prompt begins with the literal line `TASK: orientation`. To the real model it is the start of an instruction. To FakeLLM it is a routing key. Claude wrote this prompt in the 2026-11 commits, and the marker is part of the contract between prompts and the fake."},{"file":"udebt/orientation.py","start_line":130,"end_line":140,"narration":"Units that have no cached orientation are batched into one call. It passes the prompt and schema, `cwd=root` (the analysed repo) and `read_tools=True`, so the model can read the code it describes. The call goes through the LLM interface rather than a specific backend."},{"file":"udebt/llm.py","start_line":54,"end_line":66,"narration":"These constants carry the decision. A Read deny rule on `.udebt/` keeps reference answers and grades away from the model. ISOLATION loads only user settings and strict MCP, so the cloned repo's hooks and `.mcp.json` cannot run. UDEBT_NOTE repeats the ban in the prompt."},{"file":"udebt/llm.py","start_line":89,"end_line":126,"narration":"`_run` builds the `claude -p` command with isolation and the JSON schema. It offers only Read, Glob and Grep (`--tools`), allows only those, and adds the deny rule. Any non-zero exit, non-JSON output or `is_error` becomes an `LLMError`. It runs with cwd in the repo, so the flags are what provide the isolation."},{"file":"udebt/llm.py","start_line":216,"end_line":229,"narration":"In fake mode the same call is answered here. The `TASK: orientation` marker selects a canned reply built from the unit blocks parsed out of the prompt. It deliberately includes an invented path so callers' validation is exercised. An unmatched prompt raises `LLMError`."}],"trap":"A reader may think the repo's .udebt/ protection and isolation come from running the CLI in a different directory, but the call runs with cwd inside the analysed repo, and only the flags (user-only settings, strict MCP, named tools, Read deny rule) keep the repo out.","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788"},"json-contract":{"flow":"A developer runs `udebt push` to upload their repo's understanding history to the hosted store, and later `udebt pull` to bring back evidence recorded on the host.","steps":[{"file":"udebt/contract.py","start_line":356,"end_line":374,"narration":"Before anything leaves the machine, the map is cut down. `sanitize_map` keeps a short list of top-level keys and copies each unit minus `walkthrough`, `questions`, `provenance` and `churn`, recursing into children. That drop is how reference answers and line-range narration stay local, as Claude decided in commit 12ee384 on 2026-11-15. It is a denylist, so any new unit field added later will upload unless someone adds it to `UNIT_DROP`."},{"file":"udebt/remote.py","start_line":108,"end_line":118,"narration":"`payload` builds what gets sent. It brings the snapshot series up to date, refuses to push if replay left a note, takes the export, and swaps in the sanitized map. The last line runs `contract.check(\"import\", ...)`, so the CLI validates its own output and fails locally with a ContractError instead of sending a body the host would reject."},{"file":"udebt/contract.py","start_line":271,"end_line":306,"narration":"This is the stdlib validator that `check` relies on. Types, required keys, enums and ranges are enforced for the properties a schema names. `additionalProperties` defaults to True at line 296, so unknown keys are ignored. That default is the code behind the 'open schema, bump only on removal or retype' decision: adding a field breaks no consumer."},{"file":"udebt/contract.py","start_line":315,"end_line":353,"narration":"`validate` runs the schema, then for export/import kinds only, cross-field rules a schema can't express. Snapshot developers must appear in `devs`, risk-event units must appear in `units`, and a `contract` newer than `CONTRACT` is rejected. 'import' maps to the same EXPORT schema, so the push body and `udebt export` share one frozen shape."},{"file":"udebt/remote.py","start_line":121,"end_line":135,"narration":"`push` loads the URL config, builds and validates the payload, serializes it, and computes counts. With dry-run it stops there and reports `sent: False`. Otherwise it POSTs to `<url>/import`. The token is read only at that moment. Idempotence is not enforced here. The module docstring says the host keys rows naturally, so a repeated push adds nothing."},{"file":"udebt/remote.py","start_line":145,"end_line":176,"narration":"`pull` is the return path. It refuses a host whose contract is newer than `CONTRACT`. It validates each evidence row individually and counts failures as `invalid` instead of aborting. Rows whose natural key is already local are skipped, and the rest are appended with `origin: remote`. Pulled snapshots overwrite a gitignored file, so repeated pulls don't duplicate evidence."}],"trap":"A reader may assume the contract validator is strict about the whole payload and that `sanitize_map` is an allowlist. The validator ignores unknown properties, and `sanitize_map` only drops four named unit fields, so a new sensitive unit field would be uploaded unless it is added to `UNIT_DROP`.","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788"},"hosted-analysis":{"flow":"A signed-in person POSTs a public GitHub repo URL to /analyses with their session token and their own Anthropic key. The request goes through a background job that clones, runs the model tool loop and writes the result to Supabase.","steps":[{"file":"udebt/hosted/worker_service.py","start_line":420,"end_line":434,"narration":"The stdlib `ThreadingHTTPServer` handler is the entry point. It rejects bodies over 16 KB, parses the JSON, pulls the bearer token and the `X-Anthropic-Key` header, and calls `service.submit`. A good request gets a 202 straight away, not the finished analysis. Claude chose a plain stdlib server on 2026-11-15 so the package keeps no web-framework dependency."},{"file":"udebt/hosted/worker_service.py","start_line":244,"end_line":266,"narration":"After validating the URL, rev and model, `_who` checks the token against Supabase. Under a lock, a second request while a job is running gets 429. This is the one-analysis-at-a-time rule for the 512 MB free instance. Otherwise a `Job` is created and a `jobs` row is written with the person's token. Then a `MessagesLLM` is built around the person's key and a daemon thread starts, so the HTTP call returns the job id while the work continues."},{"file":"udebt/hosted/worker_service.py","start_line":286,"end_line":306,"narration":"The thread starts with cheap checks that avoid a clone. A GitHub API call rejects private or oversized repositories. `git ls-remote` resolves the sha. If that sha already has an analysis, the repo is just added to the person's list and the job finishes. Analyses of public repos are shared across users, so this is also the cost-saving path."},{"file":"udebt/worker.py","start_line":305,"end_line":321,"narration":"This is the CLI code reused in-process. `run` makes a temporary directory, clones within the limits, builds or loads the map, and calls the model for specs and walkthroughs. The `model` argument is the hosted tool loop standing in for the CLI's LLM, and `export=False` skips the slow replay. The `finally` at the end of this function deletes the clone, so the code never stays on the server."},{"file":"udebt/hosted/messages_llm.py","start_line":381,"end_line":400,"narration":"Each model request is one Messages API call. The model may call read-only repo tools (read, glob, grep), and the loop feeds the results back. It ends when the model calls `submit`. The last turn offers only `submit`, and `max_turns` caps the loop (8 by default) because each turn resends the whole conversation. The loop raises an error if the answer is cut at `max_tokens`."},{"file":"udebt/hosted/worker_service.py","start_line":345,"end_line":357,"narration":"Known failures (worker, LLM, Supabase or load errors) become a job with status failed and the error text. Any other exception is logged and shown as a generic failure, so a job never takes the service down. The `finally` removes the output directory, drops the transport so the person's key is not kept, and clears `current`. That releases the busy lock so the next request can run."}],"trap":"A reader may assume the POST runs the analysis and returns the result, or that the service stores the Anthropic key. In fact the POST returns 202 with a job id and the work runs in a background thread. The caller polls GET /analyses/<job>. The key lives only in memory for that job and is dropped in the `finally`.","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788"},"supabase-schema":{"flow":"An admin loads the mlflow sample repository's worker output into the hosted database (via the samples module and the SQL loader), after which a signed-in person opens the app and reads that shared analysis.","steps":[{"file":"udebt/hosted/samples.py","start_line":41,"end_line":65,"narration":"The flow starts when an admin runs the samples module on a worker output directory. Before any SQL is written, `problems` gates the output: it must be the pinned commit, have a map, carry no code excerpts or walkthrough code lines, and have units. This keeps stored sample content to references only, which matters because the admin workflow's logs are public."},{"file":"udebt/hosted/samples.py","start_line":105,"end_line":116,"narration":"If the output passes, the sample's URL and licence come from the hard-coded SAMPLES table rather than the manifest, and the result is written to a `<name>.sql` file with its size and sha256 printed. The file is a reviewable artefact that the owner approves and the Plabs session runs through the admin workflow. Claude wrote this on 2026-11-15 (commit a141646)."},{"file":"udebt/loadsql.py","start_line":91,"end_line":101,"narration":"`analysis_rows` normalises the URL (trailing slash and .git removed) and, for public loads, enforces a github.com pattern. It then drops the per-developer export and strips commit emails from specs and map. The loader therefore decides what leaves the worker's output, not the schema."},{"file":"udebt/loadsql.py","start_line":158,"end_line":180,"narration":"`analysis_sql` emits one transaction. The repository is upserted by URL, then the analysis for (repository, sha, owner) is deleted and re-inserted. Running the script twice leaves one row, which is the idempotence the decision relies on. The owner clause is `user_id is null` for a shared sample or `user_id = <uuid>` for a person's own analysis, so a sample reload never touches personal analyses."},{"file":"supabase/migrations/0001_app_udebt.sql","start_line":39,"end_line":52,"narration":"This is where the row lands. `user_id` null means shared. The `unique nulls not distinct (repo_id, sha, user_id)` constraint makes two shared analyses of the same commit impossible, which backs up the loader's delete-then-insert. The jsonb columns hold the manifest, map and specs."},{"file":"supabase/migrations/0001_app_udebt.sql","start_line":103,"end_line":114,"narration":"These policies are why the sample is readable and not writable. Any signed-in person can select rows where `user_id` is null or is their own. The insert, update and delete policies require `user_id` to equal the caller's `auth.uid()`, so no app token can create or change a shared row. Only the admin workflow, which bypasses RLS, can. This is the Supabase feature (RLS plus auth) that the decision was made for."}],"trap":"A reader may assume apps or the loader's SQL can be tested against Postgres RLS using the SQLite twin. The twin only checks the schema, constraints, cascades and loader idempotence. The RLS policies and the anon revoke exist only in the Postgres migration, and only the Postgres test covers them.","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788"},"web-app":{"flow":"A signed-in user finishes a quiz sitting in gateway mode and the browser POSTs their answers to /api/grade, which grades them through the HintWise gateway and records the sitting.","steps":[{"file":"web/src/model/client.ts","start_line":74,"end_line":86,"narration":"The browser starts here. It fetches the Supabase session token, refuses locally with a 401 if there is none, and sends the token as a Bearer header. A 429 with error 'cap' becomes a CapError so the UI can name the exhausted allowance and offer the user's own key. Any other failure becomes an ApiError carrying the server's message."},{"file":"web/netlify/functions/grade.ts","start_line":1,"end_line":6,"narration":"The Netlify function is a thin shim. It binds the path /api/grade and hands the Request to the shared handler. Claude wrote this split in the 2026-11-15 commit df32766 so the same logic can run outside Netlify."},{"file":"web/dev/api-plugin.ts","start_line":16,"end_line":21,"narration":"The Vite dev and preview server maps the same URLs to the same handler functions. A Node request is turned into a web Request, so local runs and tests exercise the code Netlify runs. When no Supabase URL is set, the plugin switches on UDEBT_FAKES, so no real backend is needed."},{"file":"web/netlify/functions/lib/auth.ts","start_line":19,"end_line":44,"narration":"authorize is the only access check. It reads the repositories table with the user's own token, and row-level security rejects bad tokens and hides unknown repositories. The result is cached for five minutes per token and repository. The function holds no service secret; every database call runs as the user."},{"file":"web/netlify/functions/lib/gateway.ts","start_line":16,"end_line":48,"narration":"Grading calls the HintWise LLM gateway with the user's token. Only a whitelist of fields is forwarded, max_tokens is clamped and body size is capped. A cap response passes through unchanged, and gateway outages become 502. The app holds no model key of its own in this path."},{"file":"web/netlify/functions/lib/handlers.ts","start_line":58,"end_line":92,"narration":"This is the whole grade flow. It authorizes, grades, then records only if the client sent a record hint whose repo_id matches the authorized repository. It reads the user's prior records and writes new rows with their spaced-review state. If the prior-records read fails, grading still succeeds and recorded is null, so the page writes the rows itself."}],"trap":"A reader may assume the Netlify functions hold the model key and a privileged database credential, but they only relay the signed-in user's own token to Supabase and the gateway, and the user's bring-your-own-key mode bypasses the functions for model calls altogether.","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788"},"provenance-responsibility":{"flow":"A developer runs `udebt responsibility seed` to compute, from git history and CODEOWNERS, which units each person is expected to understand.","steps":[{"file":"udebt/provenance.py","start_line":359,"end_line":379,"narration":"Before anyone can be made responsible, every commit is classified as ai, assisted, human or bot. An agent author identity wins first, then a body saying an agent authored it, then bot identities, then trailers. This is the AI-authorship focus of the decision, and Claude (claude-code) wrote it on 2026-11-12. Because `ai` and `bot` commits are separate classes, later steps can leave them out of anyone's responsibility."},{"file":"udebt/responsibility.py","start_line":170,"end_line":185,"narration":"`footprint` turns history into a per-developer share and mass for one unit. Bot commits and undirected AI commits are skipped, so a unit only an agent touched has no footprint. A directed agent commit counts for the directing developer at half weight, which is the same idea as the own-commit prior. Weight is multiplied by the square-root attribution, so a commit that barely touched the unit adds little."},{"file":"udebt/responsibility.py","start_line":356,"end_line":379,"narration":"`seed` first builds the pool of humans, which excludes the AI and Bot labels but adds anyone named in config `directed`. It then applies CODEOWNERS: an owner with at least 30% of a unit's files owns it. Handles that cannot be mapped to an email become `pending:` entries, so the owner can fill them in later in config."},{"file":"udebt/responsibility.py","start_line":384,"end_line":408,"narration":"Footprint picks fill in for developers CODEOWNERS did not name. A developer needs the configured share threshold and at least 0.1 of weighted mass, so one typo fix is not enough. The write loop respects hand curation: a curated set is kept unless `--force` is given, and with `--force` the manual entries survive. This is the compromise between mined ownership and the rejected hand-kept ownership files."},{"file":"udebt/provenance.py","start_line":689,"end_line":705,"narration":"The sets this seeds feed the per-developer index. This is where the own-commit prior is built. A developer's own human commit counts in full, and an assisted or directed commit counts at half. Each of those resets the 'others since' counters, so later changes by other people erode that developer's prior. Commits by others, including undirected AI commits, only add to the counters."}],"trap":"A reader may assume AI-authored commits make the AI responsible for a unit. In fact `footprint` skips them, and `seed` removes the AI and Bot labels from the pool of developers, so responsibility goes only to humans (an AI commit counts only for the developer who directed it, at half weight).","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788"},"structural-discovery":{"flow":"A user runs a map of a repository, and structure.build proposes candidate units from the tracked files and commit history before any LLM sees them.","steps":[{"file":"udebt/structure.py","start_line":229,"end_line":242,"narration":"build filters out lockfiles, generated files and media. Only source files form the clustered core. It scans the core's imports, then builds the co-change graph over all non-mechanical files so docs and config still get edges. Hubs are non-code files that appear in more than 15% of co-change commits (at least 20 commits). They are pulled out here, before clustering. Claude wrote this design on 2026-11-12."},{"file":"udebt/imports.py","start_line":199,"end_line":215,"narration":"scan reads each file that has a registered parser and returns its internal and external imports. Files over the size cap, unreadable files and files with no parser are skipped silently, so they contribute no import edges. Such a file can still be clustered through co-change alone."},{"file":"udebt/structure.py","start_line":167,"end_line":202,"narration":"The co-change graph skips commits with more than 30 files and commits whose landed weight is zero (bots). Each remaining commit adds weight w/(n-1) to every pair of files it touched, so no file receives more than w from one commit. The import graph is simpler: one unit-weight edge per internal import, with no history involved."},{"file":"udebt/structure.py","start_line":243,"end_line":251,"narration":"The two graphs are merged. When both have edges, the import graph is rescaled so imports carry exactly half of the merged total weight. Without this, the number of commits would swamp the import edges. If either graph is empty, the other is used as it is. Every core file is added as a node so files with no edges become singletons, and then Louvain runs with a fixed seed."},{"file":"udebt/graph.py","start_line":163,"end_line":192,"narration":"This is Louvain in the standard library only. It repeats local moves and aggregation until modularity stops improving, using a seeded shuffle and sorted tie-breaking so the result is reproducible. Community ids are ordered by size and then by smallest node name, not by dict order."},{"file":"udebt/structure.py","start_line":252,"end_line":284,"narration":"Communities of two or more files become clusters, ranked by commit mass and named C1, C2 and so on. Non-code files are attached to the cluster they co-change with most. Any cluster with at least 8 files is run through Louvain again on its own subgraph. If that gives two or more sub-communities of size 2 or more, they become children named like C1.4. That is the two-level hierarchy."}],"trap":"A reader may assume the units are a directory grouping or that the two graphs count equally by raw weight. In fact directories only feed the labels. The import graph is rescaled to half of the merged weight, and docs and config are kept out of the clustering and attached afterwards.","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788"},"teaching-layer":{"flow":"A developer opens the page printed by `udebt serve`, loads the Review tab (GET /api/review), answers the due questions, and submits the sitting (POST /api/answers), which grades and records them and reschedules each item.","steps":[{"file":"udebt/serve.py","start_line":730,"end_line":756,"narration":"Every request goes through `_route`. It checks the Host header, then the per-run token for API calls, looks the path up in the GET or POST table, parses the JSON body for POSTs and returns the handler's dict as JSON. Failures become JSON errors: a model failure is a 502, a missing record is a 404, and anything else is a printed traceback plus a 500. This is the whole 'framework' that the stdlib-server decision swapped in."},{"file":"udebt/serve.py","start_line":377,"end_line":388,"narration":"The Review tab calls `get_review`. It reads the developer's records, asks the review module for items that are due (at most five), and turns each into a served question. `serve_question` stores it in memory under a fresh id with source 'review', so a later answer can be tied back to it. No model call is made and nothing is written."},{"file":"udebt/review.py","start_line":139,"end_line":163,"narration":"`items` and `due` are where the queue comes from. It is rebuilt each time from the stored records, not kept in a table. For each unit and level with a scheduled state, it picks the phrasing of the latest missed question (a variant if the last ask used the original). `due` then keeps those whose due time has passed. Claude wrote this in the 2026-11-14 teaching commit, on top of the existing quiz records."},{"file":"udebt/serve.py","start_line":564,"end_line":587,"narration":"When the sitting ends the page posts all its answers to `post_answers`. Questions marked 'I don't know' keep their blank locked answer whatever the client sends. An id that is not in the in-memory dict gives a 404, and the batch goes to `answered_all`. The served questions are removed only after grading succeeds, so a failed call can be retried."},{"file":"udebt/serve.py","start_line":167,"end_line":192,"narration":"`answered_all` grades the whole sitting in one model call, under the lock that serialises writers. It then groups the answers by source, forced flag and kind, and writes each group through the same `quiz.record` the CLI uses. That is why a browser answer is the same record the CLI would write. Each group re-reads the prior records so its review state is folded on top of the earlier writes."},{"file":"udebt/review.py","start_line":47,"end_line":75,"narration":"Recording calls `review.stamp` (quiz.py:555), which uses `quality` and `step`. A verdict and confidence become a 0-5 quality. A miss resets the interval to 1 day and lowers the ease. A correct answer lengthens the interval to 1 day, then 3 days, then interval times ease. A correct answer on an item that has never been missed returns None, so nothing is scheduled. That is how `get_review` finds the item again at the right time."}],"trap":"A reader may think the review queue is a stored schedule that the server updates. It is recomputed from the answer records on every request, and the only state the server keeps in memory is the set of served-but-unanswered questions.","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788"},"parity-tests":{"flow":"A maintainer changes a quiz prompt rule or a scoring constant in the Python CLI, then runs the test suites to see how the web app's TypeScript copy is kept in step.","steps":[{"file":"udebt/hosted/prompt_parts.py","start_line":22,"end_line":37,"narration":"The Python side is the source of truth. It collects the CLI's own prompt text, the ladder level names and the two JSON schemas from udebt/prompts.py and udebt/ladder.py into one dict, so the web app has no hand-written copy of the rules. Claude (claude-code) added this on 2026-11-15 in a25b473."},{"file":"udebt/hosted/prompt_parts.py","start_line":40,"end_line":45,"narration":"text() serialises the dict deterministically, and running the module as a script prints it. Redirecting that output into web/src/core/prompt-parts.json is the regeneration step. The same text() is also what the test later compares against, so the generator and the checker cannot disagree on formatting."},{"file":"tests/test_hosted_parity.py","start_line":59,"end_line":85,"narration":"For the scoring half, the Python test builds cases by running the real scoring and ladder code over fixed record sets and several share values. It stores the inputs together with the Python answers. The TypeScript port never has to guess what Python would return, because the expected values are Python's own output."},{"file":"tests/test_hosted_parity.py","start_line":204,"end_line":225,"narration":"These are the drift tripwires. The fixture test regenerates the cases and fails if the committed parity.json differs. It writes the file only when UDEBT_WRITE_PARITY=1 is set or the file is missing. The prompt-parts test fails if the committed JSON differs from text(). A change to Python behaviour therefore breaks CI until someone regenerates the files, and the regenerated files then break the TypeScript tests if the port wasn't updated."},{"file":"web/src/core/prompts.ts","start_line":93,"end_line":117,"narration":"The web functions can't run the Python CLI, so they build the quiz prompt in TypeScript. They interpolate the generated JSON strings (ladder block, one-fact rule, reference rule and so on) into a template. The surrounding template wording is hand-written TypeScript and is not covered by the generated JSON."},{"file":"web/test/parity.test.ts","start_line":34,"end_line":52,"narration":"The vitest suite loops over every Python-computed ladder case and checks that the TypeScript port gives the same level values, depth, next level, understanding, debt and source. Floats are compared to 12 decimal places. This is the second half of the contract: Python checks that the fixture is current, and TypeScript checks that its port matches the fixture."}],"trap":"A reader may assume that the two implementations are tied together by one runtime or by TypeScript tests that call Python. They are not. They are linked only by committed JSON files that Python generates, and each language's test suite checks against those files separately. So a stale file fails the Python tests, and a diverging port fails the TypeScript tests.","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788"},"drafts-hosted-api":{"flow":"A team member's browser calls GET /api/r/{org}/{repo}/dashboard with a session token against the parked hosted API running on Postgres.","steps":[{"file":"drafts/hosted-api/app.py","start_line":109,"end_line":126,"narration":"Every route is wrapped by `endpoint`. It builds a context holding the raw Authorization header, then runs the plain handler in a worker thread because the DB calls block. It also maps the draft's exceptions (Unauthorized, Forbidden, contract errors, HTTPError) to 401, 403, 422 and other statuses. The Supabase and Netlify design that replaced this draft has no server layer like this."},{"file":"drafts/hosted-api/app.py","start_line":281,"end_line":286,"narration":"The dashboard handler calls `_read`, which opens two transactions. The first is the service role, used only to work out who the caller is. The second is opened as that user, so Postgres row-level security applies. This split is the draft's central idea: reads run under the caller's own identity and writes run as the service role."},{"file":"drafts/hosted-api/auth.py","start_line":67,"end_line":86,"narration":"The bearer header is turned into a user row. Tokens starting `udbt_` are looked up by SHA-256 hash. Anything else is verified as an HS256 session JWT, and its `sub` claim must still match a user. A missing, malformed or unknown token raises Unauthorized, which `endpoint` turns into a 401."},{"file":"drafts/hosted-api/db.py","start_line":122,"end_line":147,"narration":"With a user id on Postgres, `tx` runs `set local role authenticated` and sets `request.jwt.claims` with that user's `sub`, imitating Supabase. SQLite takes the first branch instead, with no role switch. So row-level security is never exercised in local runs or tests, and only exists in Postgres."},{"file":"drafts/hosted-api/migrations/postgres/0002_rls.sql","start_line":46,"end_line":60,"narration":"`udebt_sees_dev` is the permission model written as SQL. Owners and leads see every developer. A member sees their own developer key's rows, or everyone's if the repo opened visibility. Viewers see no individuals. It reads the caller from the claims that `db.tx` set."},{"file":"drafts/hosted-api/migrations/postgres/0002_rls.sql","start_line":144,"end_line":155,"narration":"The snapshot, evidence and risk policies call that function, so the same query returns different rows per caller. The dashboard code never filters by role in Python. Claude parked this draft in one commit on 2026-11-15, as a reference for how these policies were written."}],"trap":"A reader may assume this is the live backend because it is wired up end to end, with routes, auth and migrations. It is parked reference code, superseded by HOSTING.md. It imports from `..` and `udebt.hosted`, so it is not a runnable package where it sits under drafts/.","head":"e9c1c8c5754f8f4e9061e4d0316c060bd8d1b788"}}}$uf10eaba9$::jsonb,
    null);
commit;
